  "summary": "Learned and physics-based models now extract organelle-, histology-, and section-level information from existing multimodal imaging and omics data.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is computational inference that bridges measurement modalities. An AI pipeline registers NanoSIMS chemical images to electron microscopy ultrastructure, mapping organelle-level distribution of therapeutics within cells. SPOmiAlign uses a feature-matching foundation model to align multimodal spatial omics sections in a modality-agnostic manner, while ST2HE generates virtual H&E images from high-resolution spatial transcriptomics data. The lung digital twin is a separate application of model-based inference: built from the largest clinical ex vivo lung perfusion dataset, it models over 75 parameters and predicts alteplase therapeutic efficacy. What is new is reuse of learned or physics-based models to extract additional information from data already collected.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
